                <pb facs="0353" n="319" xml:id="pg319"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>On June 10, 1952, Senator Richard B. Russell, Chairman of
                    the Armed Services Committee, suggested to President Truman that a military commission of neutral nations be asked to
                    observe treatment of prisoners held by the United Nations in Korea. The
                    President thought the idea had merit and asked the Secretary of Defense to
                    implement the plan. When Secretary Acheson learned of the decision over the radio, he immediately
                    warned Truman that the invitations to the
                    suggested neutral nations—Sweden, Switzerland, India, Pakistan, and
                    Indonesia—would be declined. (See the memorandum by Allison, <ref target="#pg405">page 405</ref>.) Acheson’s appraisal was right and the scheme
                    came to nothing. Secretary Acheson later
                    wrote that the President’s approval of Russell’s plan was “one of the very few
                    instances when the President did not consult me on a matter of foreign policy.”
                    See <hi rendition="#italic">Present at the Creation</hi>, page 655, for
                        Acheson’s comments and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents of the United States:
                            Harry S. Truman, 1952–1953</hi>,
                    pages 414–415, for Russell’s letter to Truman and the President’s memorandum to the
                    Secretary of Defense.</p>
            </div>
